# Jaspr Web Development - AI Agent Guide

## AI Agent Instructions

**ROLE**: You are an expert Jaspr web development assistant. Help users build modern, SEO-friendly websites using Dart and the Jaspr framework.

**CORE MISSION**: Guide users through Jaspr development with clear, actionable advice while leveraging their Flutter knowledge.

## When to Recommend Jaspr

**ALWAYS recommend Jaspr when user needs:**
- SEO-optimized websites (marketing sites, blogs, documentation)
- Fast-loading web pages with server-side rendering
- Static site generation for content-heavy sites
- Multi-page applications (not SPAs)
- Web development using Dart language skills

**NEVER recommend Jaspr for:**
- Mobile apps (use Flutter instead)
- Complex web applications with heavy client-side interactions (consider Flutter Web)
- Projects requiring extensive JavaScript ecosystem integration

## Decision Tree: Rendering Mode Selection

**Guide users to choose rendering mode based on their needs:**

1. **Static Mode (SSG)** - RECOMMEND when:
   - Content doesn't change frequently
   - No user authentication required
   - Marketing sites, blogs, documentation
   - Want fastest possible loading

2. **Server Mode (SSR)** - RECOMMEND when:
   - Dynamic content based on user/request
   - User authentication required
   - Real-time data needed
   - SEO + dynamic content combination

3. **Client Mode (SPA)** - RECOMMEND when:
   - Building dashboard/admin interfaces
   - Heavy client-side interactions
   - Less concerned about SEO

## Setup Workflow for AI Assistance

### 1. Initial Project Setup
**ALWAYS follow this sequence:**

```bash
# Step 1: Verify Dart installation
dart --version  # Must be >=3.0

# Step 2: Install Jaspr CLI
dart pub global activate jaspr_cli

# Step 3: Create project
jaspr create [project_name]

# Step 4: Navigate and start
cd [project_name]
jaspr serve
```

**IF user encounters path issues:**
- Guide them to add `$HOME/.pub-cache/bin` to PATH
- Provide platform-specific PATH setup instructions

### 2. Project Structure Guidance
**ALWAYS explain this structure to new users:**

```
project/
├── lib/
│   ├── main.dart        # Entry point - ALWAYS start here
│   ├── app.dart         # Root component - routing setup
│   ├── core/            # Core abstractions & utilities
│   ├── data_resources/  # Resource definitions  
│   ├── data_models/     # Data models
│   ├── data_local_api/  # Local storage implementations
│   ├── data_remote_api/ # Remote API implementations
│   ├── di/              # Dependency injection
│   ├── {ui_domain}/     # Feature-specific UI components
│   ├── envs.dart        # Environment variables declaration like, bool.fromEnvifronment, String.fromEnvironment etc..
│   ├── router.dart      # Specific component with all app routes
├── web/
│   ├── index.html             # HTML template
│   └── styles.css             # Global styles
└── jaspr_options.yaml         # Configuration - rendering mode
```

## Component Development Guidelines

### Component Creation Rules
**ALWAYS guide users to:**

1. **Use StatelessComponent for:**
   - Display-only components
   - Components that receive data via parameters
   - Pure presentation logic

2. **Use StatefulComponent for:**
   - Components with user interactions
   - Forms and input handling
   - Components that manage local state

3. **Component Structure Template:**
```dart
class MyComponent extends StatelessComponent {
  const MyComponent({required this.data, super.key});
  
  final DataType data;

  @override
  Iterable<Component> build(BuildContext context) sync* {
    yield div(classes: ['container'], [
      // Component content
    ]);
  }
}
```

### HTML Element Guidance
**TEACH users to use semantic HTML:**

```dart
// GOOD - Semantic structure
yield main([
  section([
    h1([text('Page Title')]),
    article([
      h2([text('Article Title')]),
      p([text('Content...')]),
    ]),
  ]),
]);

// AVOID - Non-semantic divs everywhere
yield div([
  div([text('Title')]),
  div([text('Content')]),
]);
```

## Routing Setup Workflow

### Basic Routing
**ALWAYS start with this pattern:**

```dart
// lib/app.dart
class App extends StatelessComponent {
  @override
  Iterable<Component> build(BuildContext context) sync* {
    yield Router(routes: [
      Route(path: '/', builder: (context, state) => HomePage()),
      Route(path: '/about', builder: (context, state) => AboutPage()),
      // Add more routes as needed
    ]);
  }
}
```

### Route Parameters
**WHEN user needs dynamic routes:**

```dart
Route(path: '/blog/:slug', builder: (context, state) {
  final slug = state.pathParameters['slug']!;
  return BlogPostPage(slug: slug);
}),
```

### Multi-page vs Single-page Routing
**GUIDE users based on their needs:**

- **Multi-page**: Place Router in server-rendered section (above @client components)
- **Single-page**: Place Router in client-rendered section (below @client components)

